{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "process-crankcase-running-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d: the `process-crankcase-running` RunSpec -- THC, CO and NOx running exhaust and the three crankcase processes chained from them -- computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE. It is the tenth RunSpec this chain reproduces, and the second whose output is ragged: 684 rows as 124 + 124 + 124 + 104 + 104 + 104.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES (§23), beyond being a third chain table.\n\n* A RATIO OF EXACTLY ZERO IS A RATIO. `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` carries `crankcaseRatio` = 0.0 for DIESEL model years 2001 and later, and MOVES emits those 19 cohorts, as zeros. The first port of this run tested `rt_chainRatio > 0` and emitted 627 rows instead of 684 -- 85 cohorts per crankcase block instead of 104 -- which is the same mistake as reading an unrated cohort off a rate of zero (conventions §69). The test is PRESENCE, `rt_hasChainRatio`, and this is the only run in the corpus that can tell the two apart.\n\n* THE REGULATORY CLASS IS IN THE KEY AND THIS RUN CANNOT SEE IT. `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` is keyed by regClassID as well; source type 21 has exactly one regulatory class, 20, so dropping the key would be right here and wrong for a light commercial truck -- conventions §62.3's width-one trap, named rather than closed, because no RunSpec in this directory selects a crankcase process on a multi-class source type.\n\n* THE PARENT BLOCKS ARE THE SAME 124 AS `expand-criteria`'S, to the digit. Three of the six blocks here are that document's three, and they come out at 7.805e-06, which is its number. That is the check that the chain change under them moved nothing.\n\n684 rows, worst cell 7.805e-06. Reproduced in pandas from the default database BEFORE the port, at 6.265e-06 on the chained half.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "process-crankcase-running",
      "crankcase",
      "chained",
      "ragged"
    ]
  },
